course: everyday
lesson: 264
title: Routines, screen time and earning money
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A regular evening routine — bath or shower, pyjamas, teeth, reading, then lights off at the same time — helps you fall asleep and feel fresh at school, and keeping weekend bedtimes close to normal makes Monday easier.
explain: Games and video apps are designed to keep you watching: autoplay starts the next video and «just one more level» never ends, so you agree a time, use a timer, and stop when it rings.
explain: Online, people may not be who they say they are, so you never share your real name, school, address or photos, and you tell a parent if anyone makes you uncomfortable.
explain: Money comes from work: adults earn a salary by working, and it pays for the home, food and bills, not the card machine.
explain: A boy in Colorado runs a lemonade stand with his dad: they wash their hands, price the cups clearly, and he learns to count change and talk to customers.
fact: An evening routine of bath, pyjamas, teeth, reading and lights off at the same time helps you fall asleep and feel fresh.
fact: Keeping weekend and holiday bedtimes close to normal makes going back to school easier.
fact: Games and videos are designed to keep you watching, so you stop when the agreed time is up, using a timer.
fact: Autoplay keeps you watching longer than you planned, so you turn it off or stop after your chosen video.
fact: Feeling cross when the screen goes off passes quickly if you take a few breaths and do something else.
fact: In online games you never share your real name, school, address or photos.
fact: You tell a parent if anyone online makes you uncomfortable, because people online may not be who they say they are.
fact: Doing a chore properly builds trust, and you learn useful skills.
fact: A lemonade stand or bake sale is run with an adult's help, with clean hands and clear prices.
fact: Adults earn money by working, and their salary pays for the home, food and bills.
fact: You reply to a party invitation by the date asked (invitations often say «RSVP», which means please reply), because the host needs to know how many are coming.
exercise: Which is a good evening routine? | Bath, pyjamas, teeth, reading, lights off at the same time | Screens in bed until you fall asleep | A different bedtime every night | Sweets after brushing your teeth
exercise: Why go to bed at the same time each night? | It helps you fall asleep and feel fresh at school | It makes the night shorter and the day longer | It stops you from dreaming | It is only to please your parents
exercise: It is the summer holidays. What is wise about bedtime? | Keep it close to the normal one | Stay up as late as you like every night | Go to bed at sunrise on the weekends | Sleep only in the afternoons instead
exercise: Your agreed game time is over, but you want "just one more level". What do you do? | Stop when the timer rings | Play one more, then one more | Hide the timer so it can't ring | Ask for another hour every time
exercise: Why do games make it hard to stop? | They are designed to keep you playing | They break if you stop suddenly in the middle | Your progress is always lost | They can only be paused at night
exercise: A video app starts the next video automatically. What can you do? | Turn off autoplay or stop after your chosen video | Watch until the app stops by itself, as it always does | Turn the volume down and keep watching | Let it play while you sleep
exercise: Your mum switches off the TV and you feel cross. What helps? | Take a few breaths and do something else | Shout until she switches it back on again | Sulk for the rest of the day in your room | Hide the remote control so she can't switch it off
exercise: A player in an online game asks which school you go to. What do you do? | Do not tell them; tell a parent if it worries you | Tell them, since they seem friendly and play fair | Send them a photo of your uniform | Tell them your home address instead of your school's name
exercise: Why should you not share your real name in online games? | People online may not be who they say they are | Real names are too long for games | Games only allow made-up names | Your friends will laugh at your name when they see it on the screen
exercise: You do not like drying dishes. What is right? | Do it properly anyway, without moaning | Do it badly so you are never asked again | Break a plate on purpose so you get sent away | Leave the dishes wet in the cupboard to dry
exercise: What does a lemonade stand teach you? | Earning, counting money and talking to customers | How to get rich without doing any work at all | How to cheat customers | How to make lemons grow faster
exercise: You want to run a bake sale. What should you do? | Do it with an adult, wash hands and price things clearly | Run it alone at night | Sell food without washing your hands | Charge whatever each customer looks able to pay, rich or poor
exercise: Where does the money in your family's bank account come from? | From adults working and earning a salary | From the card machine in the shop | From the bank printing it for families | From the government for free to everyone
exercise: You are invited to a party with "Reply by Friday". What do you do? | Reply by Friday to say yes or no | Reply on the day of the party | Not reply if you are going | Reply only if the host asks again
test: A girl in Phoenix goes to bed at 9 on school nights but at midnight every weekend. Why is Monday hard for her? | Very late weekend nights make school mornings hard | Mondays are always hard for everyone | Weekends are too short | She went to bed too early on Sunday, so she woke up in the night
test: Ten minutes of cartoons turns into two hours because the next episode keeps starting. What caused this? | Autoplay keeps you watching longer than you planned | The cartoons were too short | The remote control was broken | The TV was set too loud, so it would not let him switch it off
test: A "kid" in an online game in Canada asks for a photo of you in your school uniform. What should you do? | Refuse and tell a parent | Send it, as you are both kids | Send a photo without your face | Ask for his photo first, to check he's real
test: Why might a parent trust you with bigger jobs if you always do small chores properly? | Doing a job well builds trust | Parents only trust children who complain | Small chores do not matter to anyone at all | Doing chores badly shows you are busy
test: A little boy thinks money comes out of the card machine at the supermarket. What is true? | Money comes from adults working; the card only moves it | The card machine makes money for shoppers | Banks give money to everyone who asks | The supermarket pays families to do their shopping there every week
test: A boy feels angry when his game time ends. Which is the best way to handle it? | Breathe, then move on to another activity | Throw the controller | Argue for an extra hour until his parents say yes | Refuse to eat dinner
test: A girl in Wales sells cupcakes at the school fair. Why does she wash her hands and label the prices clearly? | It keeps food safe and customers know what to pay | Labels make cupcakes taste better | Washing hands makes them sell faster than the other stalls | It is only for show
test: Why is it hard to stop after "just one more level"? | Games are designed to keep you playing | Levels are too easy to stop | The game is always nearly finished | The timer on the game is usually wrong
test: Two days before a party, Tom still has not answered the invitation that said "RSVP by Monday". Why is that a problem? | The host does not know how many children are coming | The party will be cancelled | The invitation runs out | Tom will have to bring two presents because he answered late
test: one more vid pls!! autoplay already started the next one | Stop when the agreed time is up, turn off autoplay | Watch until autoplay ends | Just one more, then ten more | Hide the timer so nobody knows how long you've been on
test: someone in the game asked what school i go to | Never share it, and tell a parent | Tell them, they seem nice | Tell them only the town | Send a photo of your uniform instead
test: party invite says reply by friday, its thursday, meh i'll just turn up | Reply by the date so the host knows how many are coming | Just turn up, it's fine | Don't reply and don't go | Reply after the party
test: weekends i wanna stay up till 1 lol | Keep weekend bedtimes close to normal | Stay up all weekend, then catch up by sleeping on Monday | Bedtime only matters on school days | Sleep all Saturday
explain: A boy in Ohio called Josh watched his tablet until midnight every school night; he started falling asleep in class and his marks dropped a whole grade.
explain: A girl in England answered an online job advert for kids "earning £50 a day"; it was a scam that asked for her parents' bank details, and her family lost money.
fact: Screens late at night make it harder to fall asleep, and children who sleep too little learn less and are more irritable (easily cross).
fact: Online offers that promise children easy money are almost always scams.
test: watching vids in bed till 12, it's fine i'm not tired | Switch off and sleep, tiredness catches up | Fine, kids don't need much sleep | Fine if the screen is turned down dim | Only on school nights
test: ad says kids can earn $50 a day, just need ur parents bank info | It's a scam, tell an adult | Send it, it's quick money | Send only the card number | Ask a friend to send theirs
test: Why did Josh's marks drop? | He was too tired from late screen time | The work got easier | He was too clever | His teacher changed
test: did u hear a kid told some guy in an online game which school he went to and the guy turned up at the gate? real lesson? | Never share your name, school or address online | Share only if he seems friendly | Tell him only your school, never your address | Online friends are always real kids
test: apparently some kid watched YouTube on autoplay for like 9 hours straight and didn't notice. what's the true lesson? | Autoplay keeps you watching; turn it off or stop | Autoplay saves you time | Nine hours is a normal amount | Watching more videos makes you smarter, whatever they are
test: my mate says there's a website paying kids 100 bucks a day for just clicking stuff. right? | Almost certainly a scam, so don't sign up | Yes, sign up straight away | Yes, if it looks professional | Yes, give them your parents' details
exam: Identify the information that is never shared in online games. | One's real name, school, address or photographs | One's favourite colour | One's chosen game character | One's high score on the leaderboard of the game
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the design of games and videos? | They are designed to keep the user watching | They are designed to make the user stop quickly | They are designed to improve sleep | They are designed for short use only
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice for a party invitation. | A reply is given by the date requested | A reply is unnecessary | A reply is given on the day of the party | A reply is given only if attending
exam: Ascertain the effect of screens late at night upon children. | Sleep is more difficult, and learning and mood suffer | Sleep is deeper and longer | They have no effect on sleep | They improve learning and concentration the next day
